=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The network is operating exactly as designed. PC4 and PC5's inability to reach
Location C (172.18.50.0/24 and 172.18.51.0/24 on mswitch2) is not caused by
any routing failure on router0 or anywhere else — all RIP daemons are healthy,
all FRR processes are running, and router0's routing table contains correct
learned routes to every other site in the topology. PC4 can successfully ping
Location D hosts (pc13 at 192.168.200.13) with 0% loss, proving that the
"outside network" is not down.

The Location C subnets are unreachable because router6 carries a deliberate
inbound ACL on its WAN-facing interface (eth2, the link to router3). This
iptables FORWARD chain — present verbatim in /hostlab/router6.startup —
drops all ICMP, all TCP (except HTTP/80 from 172.16.10.0/24 to 172.18.51.1),
and all UDP that arrives on eth2. Traffic from pc4 to pc15 travels through
router0→router1→router2→router3 and arrives at router6 on eth2, where it is
silently discarded by the final catch-all DROP rule. The three ICMP packets
from our diagnostic ping were confirmed dropped (iptables counter: 3 packets,
252 bytes). This ACL is an intentional security policy applied to the serial
WAN uplink to restrict inbound access into Location C's networks. The user's
premise — that the router "forgot every route" — is incorrect; the routing
plane is fully converged and no corrective action is needed.
=== END ===
